WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.600
All right, everyone.

00:04.600 --> 00:06.440
So now we have this very simple example.

00:06.440 --> 00:08.440
We have the months and the cells.

00:08.480 --> 00:14.080
Now we can use the value functions in order to access a value from another row.

00:14.080 --> 00:19.600
So in order to understand it let's say that SQL processing the months and we are currently at the month

00:19.600 --> 00:20.400
of March.

00:20.400 --> 00:25.640
So now for example, I would like to access the value from the previous month from February.

00:25.640 --> 00:30.840
So in order to do that, we can use the lag function in order to get the value of ten.

00:30.840 --> 00:36.960
So with that we have in the same row the current sales of the month March, and as well the sales from

00:36.960 --> 00:39.080
the previous month, the February.

00:39.080 --> 00:44.120
And maybe in other cases, I would like to get the sales of the next month from April.

00:44.240 --> 00:50.520
In order to do that, we can use the function lead and we will get at the same row the value five.

00:50.560 --> 00:56.400
So now I can very quickly compare the current month with the previous month, and as well with the next

00:56.400 --> 00:56.880
month.

00:56.920 --> 01:01.240
And now in the other cases, you might be interested in the first month of your list.

01:01.240 --> 01:03.000
So it's going to be here January.

01:03.000 --> 01:08.840
So in order to get the sales of the first month, you can use the function first value.

01:08.840 --> 01:11.440
So we're going to get at the same row 20.

01:11.480 --> 01:13.770
And now for the last option I think you already get it.

01:13.810 --> 01:17.610
We can go and get the value of sales of the last month.

01:17.610 --> 01:19.130
So here we can get the July.

01:19.170 --> 01:21.530
So for that we're going to use the function last value.

01:21.650 --> 01:23.970
And we will get the value of 40.

01:24.010 --> 01:28.290
So this is exactly the purpose of the value functions or analytical functions.

01:28.290 --> 01:31.570
We can access a value from another rows.

01:31.610 --> 01:33.370
And here it's important to understand as well.

01:33.370 --> 01:35.930
The value functions is like the ranking functions.

01:35.930 --> 01:41.370
We have to use the order by in order to sort the data in order to understand what is the first row and

01:41.370 --> 01:42.130
the last row.

01:42.130 --> 01:44.530
In this example the data is sorted by the month.

01:44.530 --> 01:47.690
So guys the access functions are really important for analytics.

01:47.690 --> 01:52.730
You can use it in order to access a value from other rows in order to do comparison.

01:52.770 --> 01:53.050
All right.

01:53.050 --> 01:57.650
So now let's have a quick overview of the syntax and the rules for the value functions.

01:57.650 --> 02:01.890
So here we have four functions lead lag first value and last value.

02:02.130 --> 02:04.610
So as you can see we can group them into two groups.

02:04.610 --> 02:05.850
So we have the lead and lag.

02:05.890 --> 02:08.730
They are very similar to each other especially with the syntax.

02:08.730 --> 02:11.730
We can use three things or three arguments inside it.

02:11.770 --> 02:14.850
Expression offset default for both of them.

02:15.010 --> 02:17.850
For the first value we can use only an expression.

02:17.850 --> 02:21.130
So that means we have to pass a value for those functions.

02:21.130 --> 02:22.450
You cannot leave it empty.

02:22.450 --> 02:23.970
So now about the expression data type.

02:23.970 --> 02:26.340
You can use any field with any data type.

02:26.340 --> 02:28.020
There is no restrictions about only.

02:28.020 --> 02:31.140
For example, using numbers, any data type is allowed.

02:31.180 --> 02:32.900
Now about the definition of the window.

02:33.260 --> 02:35.220
The partition p as usual is optional.

02:35.220 --> 02:38.500
Like any other group, the order by here is a must.

02:38.540 --> 02:40.460
You must define an order by.

02:40.460 --> 02:41.740
It's like the ranking.

02:41.900 --> 02:43.460
So here you cannot leave it empty.

02:43.540 --> 02:44.780
Now we come to the last one.

02:44.780 --> 02:46.140
We have the frame clause.

02:46.300 --> 02:48.100
There are really different stuff over here.

02:48.100 --> 02:53.180
So for the first two functions, lead and lag, you are not allowed to define any frame.

02:53.180 --> 02:55.580
So you are not allowed to define any subset of data.

02:55.820 --> 02:57.540
It's very similar to the ranking.

02:57.540 --> 02:59.820
So you must use order by.

02:59.860 --> 03:02.140
But you cannot define the frame of the window.

03:02.140 --> 03:06.300
But for the other two functions, the first value and the last value, they are optional.

03:06.300 --> 03:12.260
You can go and use them, and for the last value it is recommended to define a frame clause.

03:12.300 --> 03:12.980
Don't worry about it.

03:12.980 --> 03:15.060
We're going to have enough examples in order to understand.

03:15.060 --> 03:17.660
So as you can see those functions has different requirements.

03:17.660 --> 03:20.300
So there is no generic rule for all of them.

03:20.300 --> 03:24.260
But one thing that they all agree on that you must use order by.

03:24.300 --> 03:28.420
So now as usual, what we're going to do, we're going to go and deep dive into those functions.

03:28.420 --> 03:33.180
We're going to address first the two functions lead and lag, because they are very similar to each

03:33.220 --> 03:33.700
others.

